> Yes, if you modify the response it will be directly taken in account for the hits. I made an example for you here:
> 
> https://codesandbox.io/s/instantsearchjs-app-l3dft
> 
> ```js
> const searchClient = {
>   search: function(requests) {
>     return algoliaSearchClient.search(requests).then(content => {
>       // do your api call here
>       const hits = content.results[0].hits;
>       console.log(hits);
>       return $.ajax('https://httpbin.org/anything', {
>         method: 'POST',
>         content: { hits },
>       }).then(res => {
>         // return from request
>         // content.results[0].hits = res
>         return content;
>       });
>     });
>   },
>   searchForFacetValues: function(requests) {
>     return algoliaSearchClient.searchForFacetValues(requests);
>   },
> };
> ```
> 
> as you can see, you need to make sure you "modify" the response from the regular js client

Thank you so much this worked
